1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,327
Hi — this is Bella, for Chaitanya.

2
00:00:02,367 --> 00:00:03,100
A bug report:

3
00:00:03,567 --> 00:00:05,833
the Logout button logs nobody out.

4
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:08,933
Every user, every time,
and only in production.

5
00:00:09,633 --> 00:00:13,660
This is the postmortem — what broke,
and why it was invisible where you'd

6
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:14,040
look.

7
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:15,573
Someone clicks Logout.

8
00:00:15,613 --> 00:00:16,947
The page redirects.

9
00:00:17,147 --> 00:00:18,447
And they're still signed in.

10
00:00:19,047 --> 00:00:23,173
Not a rare edge case, not a flaky one —
every time, for everyone, on the

11
00:00:23,213 --> 00:00:23,913
live site.

12
00:00:24,547 --> 00:00:27,113
The button does something;
it just doesn't do what it says.

13
00:00:27,430 --> 00:00:28,963
And here's what makes it interesting.

14
00:00:29,697 --> 00:00:33,397
The same code, on a developer's
laptop, logged out perfectly.

15
00:00:33,930 --> 00:00:37,497
It had worked for months, and nothing
in the logout code had changed.

16
00:00:37,930 --> 00:00:39,663
So before you read a line of it:

17
00:00:39,897 --> 00:00:42,097
whatever's wrong here
probably isn't the code.

18
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:44,113
On paper the button did a thorough job.

19
00:00:44,647 --> 00:00:47,213
It called the server to
record that you'd logged out.

20
00:00:47,747 --> 00:00:51,347
Then it walked every cookie it could
see and deleted it — plain domain,

21
00:00:52,047 --> 00:00:54,347
parent domain, localhost.

22
00:00:54,413 --> 00:00:58,613
Cleared local storage, cleared session
storage, and redirected you home.

23
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:02,387
What it never did was ask the
identity provider to actually end the

24
00:01:02,427 --> 00:01:03,127
session.

25
00:01:03,293 --> 00:01:07,087
It cleaned up every piece of evidence
that you were logged in, by hand,

26
00:01:07,127 --> 00:01:09,260
and assumed that was the same thing.

27
00:01:09,727 --> 00:01:11,027
For months, it was.

28
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:14,767
Then the project moved from the
provider's development setup to its

29
00:01:14,807 --> 00:01:15,673
production one.

30
00:01:16,173 --> 00:01:19,273
Different infrastructure — and a
different place to keep the session.

31
00:01:19,907 --> 00:01:21,673
Nobody touched the logout code.

32
00:01:22,307 --> 00:01:23,573
The ground moved under it.

33
00:01:23,690 --> 00:01:27,057
On the development setup the session
cookie lived on the app's own domain.

34
00:01:27,923 --> 00:01:31,557
Page JavaScript could see it,
so deleting it by hand worked.

35
00:01:32,057 --> 00:01:35,690
On production the session lives on a
different domain — the provider's own

36
00:01:35,823 --> 00:01:38,557
— and it's marked HttpOnly.

37
00:01:38,657 --> 00:01:41,257
Page JavaScript cannot
read it or delete it.

38
00:01:41,623 --> 00:01:43,923
That's not a bug;
that's the point of the flag.

39
00:01:44,523 --> 00:01:47,190
It's what stops a malicious
script stealing your session.

40
00:01:47,410 --> 00:01:51,737
So the loop ran, deleted everything
it could reach, reported success — and

41
00:01:51,777 --> 00:01:54,477
the one cookie that mattered
sat there untouched.

42
00:01:55,110 --> 00:01:57,877
Reload, and the provider
helpfully signs you back in.

43
00:01:58,510 --> 00:01:59,577
The code wasn't wrong.

44
00:02:00,243 --> 00:02:02,443
Its assumption about where
the session lived was.

45
00:02:02,660 --> 00:02:04,327
The fix is one line in spirit:

46
00:02:04,793 --> 00:02:07,253
actually call the provider's sign-out.

47
00:02:07,293 --> 00:02:08,327
But look at how it's written.

48
00:02:09,027 --> 00:02:13,353
The call is raced against a five-second
timer, and if it loses, the code

49
00:02:13,393 --> 00:02:14,527
redirects you anyway.

50
00:02:15,293 --> 00:02:19,253
Signing out is a network call to
someone else's service — if that service

51
00:02:19,293 --> 00:02:23,087
is slow, you don't want a user
stranded on a page that won't let them

52
00:02:23,127 --> 00:02:23,630
leave.

53
00:02:23,670 --> 00:02:27,463
The cookie loop stayed, by the way —
it isn't what fixes this, but it's

54
00:02:27,503 --> 00:02:29,437
harmless and it covers the local case.

55
00:02:30,103 --> 00:02:32,037
And one thing is still live as I record.

56
00:02:32,703 --> 00:02:33,837
The fix is on main.

57
00:02:34,503 --> 00:02:38,063
The development branch does not have
it yet, so the next release cut from

58
00:02:38,103 --> 00:02:40,270
development would bring
the bug straight back.

59
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:41,707
Two things to take away.

60
00:02:42,373 --> 00:02:46,073
Don't reimplement what a library
already does, especially security.

61
00:02:46,773 --> 00:02:50,773
The manual cleanup looked more thorough
than one function call; it wasn't,

62
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:52,440
it was more code doing less.

63
00:02:53,107 --> 00:02:56,967
And this bug could not be found in
development — the environments differed

64
00:02:57,007 --> 00:02:59,840
in exactly the way the broken
assumption depended on.

65
00:03:00,373 --> 00:03:01,807
That's the dangerous kind.

66
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:03,357
Let's recap with Claude.

67
00:03:03,923 --> 00:03:07,217
The button deleted every trace of
the session it could reach, and never

68
00:03:07,257 --> 00:03:08,757
asked the provider to end it.

69
00:03:09,257 --> 00:03:12,923
That worked until the session moved
to a domain the page can't touch.

70
00:03:13,257 --> 00:03:15,057
And be precise about what's fixed:

71
00:03:15,590 --> 00:03:18,423
logging out always revoked
textbook access correctly.

72
00:03:19,057 --> 00:03:22,857
It was the hub's own session that
survived, and that half is fixed on main

73
00:03:23,123 --> 00:03:24,623
— development is still waiting.

74
00:03:24,870 --> 00:03:25,570
Your turn.

75
00:03:25,837 --> 00:03:26,970
Take this into Claude:

76
00:03:27,437 --> 00:03:31,430
My logout clears cookies and local
storage by hand instead of calling my

77
00:03:31,470 --> 00:03:33,497
auth provider's sign-out.

78
00:03:33,537 --> 00:03:37,897
Tell me what that breaks once the
session cookie is HttpOnly and on the

79
00:03:37,937 --> 00:03:41,530
provider's own domain, and what else
in my app might be assuming state

80
00:03:41,570 --> 00:03:43,303
lives somewhere it no longer does.

81
00:03:43,937 --> 00:03:46,003
That last clause is the one to push on.

82
00:03:46,603 --> 00:03:48,703
This was never really
a bug about logout.

83
00:03:48,810 --> 00:03:49,977
The Logout That Didn't.

84
00:03:50,343 --> 00:03:52,410
For Chaitanya and Humanitarians AI.
